WebKit: the IPC socket monitor only waits; the reads and the close run on the connection's queue (LazyOS).

The PlayStation path's SocketMonitor thread (patch 0022 gives it a poll() loop) called readyReadHandler and,
on a hangup, connectionDidClose on its own thread, while Connection::invalidate runs platformInvalidate on
the connection's queue and sendOutgoingMessage writes on that queue too. Every other port does all of this
on the queue, and the code assumes it: platformInvalidate tests m_isConnected and then closes the descriptor
and drops m_socketMonitor, so when a page's process ended while its proxy was being shut down (a process
swap, Back to another site, a view destroyed) both threads could close the same number, the second time a
descriptor some other file or connection had taken by then, and both could drop the one reference to the
monitor's WTF::Thread. The UI process's heap went bad and webd aborted later and elsewhere: the main run
loop's timer tree deeper than 128 (RedBlackTree::insert from ResponsivenessTimer::start, under
WKPageGoBack and WKPageHandleWheelEvent, and ~TimerBase), seen in the browser monkey's runs; patches 0021
and 0027 work around crashes of the same shape (a CheckedPtr count already zero after a process ended).

The thread now only waits for the descriptor and hands what it found to the queue with dispatchSync, so the
reads, the close and platformInvalidate never run at once; it waits for the queue to finish before it polls
again (the data is read by then, so the poll does not report it a second time). A close the read did not
see is made there only while the connection still holds a descriptor.

--- a/Source/WebKit/Platform/IPC/unix/ConnectionUnix.cpp
+++ b/Source/WebKit/Platform/IPC/unix/ConnectionUnix.cpp
@@ -340,12 +340,21 @@
     m_isConnected = true;
 
 #if PLATFORM(LAZYOS)
-    // poll(), not select() on an fd_set: a long session's descriptors pass 1024 (SocketMonitorLazyOS.h).
+    // poll(), not select() on an fd_set: a long session's descriptors pass 1024 (SocketMonitorLazyOS.h). The
+    // thread only waits: the read and the close run on the connection's queue, where platformInvalidate and
+    // sendOutgoingMessage run, so no two threads close the descriptor or drop m_socketMonitor (patch 0036).
     m_socketMonitor = Thread::create("SocketMonitor"_s, [protectedThis] {
+        Ref queue = protectedThis->m_connectionQueue;
         LazyOS::monitorSocket(
             [&] { return protectedThis->socketDescriptor(); },
-            [&] { protectedThis->readyReadHandler(); },
-            [&] { protectedThis->connectionDidClose(); });
+            [&] { queue->dispatchSync([&] { protectedThis->readyReadHandler(); }); },
+            [&] {
+                queue->dispatchSync([&] {
+                    // Not when platformInvalidate ran first: the connection has let go of it.
+                    if (protectedThis->socketDescriptor() != -1)
+                        protectedThis->connectionDidClose();
+                });
+            });
     });
     return;
 #endif
